    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d339">

                <pb facs="0495" n="381" xml:id="pg381"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5605: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-10-11T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">October 11,
                                1924—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received October 11—6:18
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>383. Marshal Wu Pei-fu left Peking early this morning to direct operations at the
                    northern front. Colonel Barnard and Captain Woodbridge<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d339-f1"/> accompanied him as observers at his invitation. British,
                    French and Japanese officers also accompanied Wu in like capacity.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d339-f1" n="11" xml:id="d339-f1-content">
                <p>U. S. Army officers attached to the Legation in China.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
